<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
	<PropertyGroup>
		<TargetFramework>net10.0-ios</TargetFramework>
		<!-- Deployment floor, NOT the SDK version. Avalonia.iOS 12.1.1 ships its
		     lib under net10.0-ios26.0, which pins the SDK it is compiled against;
		     the app itself still runs back to this minimum. -->
		<SupportedOSPlatformVersion>13.0</SupportedOSPlatformVersion>
		<OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
		<Nullable>enable</Nullable>
		<ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
		<ApplicationId>com.daqifi.avalonia</ApplicationId>
		<ApplicationVersion>1</ApplicationVersion>
		<!-- CFBundleShortVersionString. From Directory.Build.props, the single place the app's
		     version is declared, so the store version and the version a crash is tagged with in
		     Sentry cannot drift apart (#126). -->
		<ApplicationDisplayVersion>$(DaqifiAppVersion)</ApplicationDisplayVersion>
		<!-- Declared so the lock file carries every RID this head is ever built
		     for. An iOS build always resolves a RID (there is no RID-less iOS
		     build), so omitting these would let the first `-r` restore prune the
		     lock file to one RID and trip NU1004 on the next — the same trap
		     Directory.Build.props documents for the Desktop head. Device
		     (ios-arm64) is listed for the eventual provisioned build; simulator
		     is what runs today.
		     iossimulator-x64 is deliberately NOT here. The .NET iOS SDK injects
		     UIRequiredDeviceCapabilities=arm64 into the built app's Info.plist
		     and that is not settable from our Info.plist (verified: absent in
		     source, present in the .app after a clean rebuild), so an x64
		     simulator RID would be a claim in tension with what the toolchain
		     actually emits — and one nobody can check, since there is no Intel
		     Mac to build it on. Add it back off the strength of a real build on
		     Intel hardware, not on the strength of it looking harmless. -->
		<RuntimeIdentifiers>iossimulator-arm64;ios-arm64</RuntimeIdentifiers>
		<!-- Interpret EF CORE ONLY, not the whole app (#102). This replaces
		     <UseInterpreter>true</UseInterpreter>, which was the same knob set to
		     "all". Still REQUIRED, still not a tuning knob: iOS is aot-only, EF Core
		     generates code at runtime, and without an interpreter the data layer
		     does not work at all.

		     What the old setting cost, measured on this head after deleting bin and
		     obj (Release, iossimulator-arm64):
		         all           app binary  6.7 MB   .app 103 MB
		         this list     app binary 35.5 MB   .app 136 MB
		                       (+ 5.6 MB aot-instances.aotdata.arm64)
		     A 6.7 MB binary is the tell: under "all" there is essentially no AOT
		     code in the app AT ALL. Avalonia's render loop, OxyPlot and our stream
		     handling were all being interpreted to keep one library working, on a
		     head whose main job is rendering a 16-channel stream at 100 Hz. Naming
		     assemblies buys that back for everything unnamed; it costs ~33 MB of
		     bundle, which is the trade being made deliberately here. The extra
		     aot-instances file is generic-instance dedup, which
		     Xamarin.Shared.Sdk.targets enables for every value EXCEPT "all".

		     Why the list is these five: they are the assemblies that build and
		     execute expression trees against our model — the query pipeline
		     (EntityFrameworkCore + Relational + Sqlite) and the bulk-insert path
		     the sample writer uses (EFCore.BulkExtensions.*). Keep it in step with
		     the EF PackageReferences in Daqifi.Avalonia.csproj.

		     What was verified is that the head works WITH this list: migration
		     applies and the Logged Data pane loads. It was NOT tested whether a
		     shorter list also works — enabling the interpreter at all is what makes
		     the SDK leave RuntimeFeature.IsDynamicCodeSupported true (see
		     Xamarin.Shared.Sdk.targets), so a shorter list may well be enough for
		     the expression trees alone. Do not shrink it on that theory without
		     running the app; EF also dispatches generically over runtime types,
		     which is the other thing an interpreter covers. Adding an assembly is
		     cheap; the failure mode for a missing one is a pane that breaks at RUN
		     time, with a green build.

		     Do NOT go back to "all" to make some other library work. Add that
		     library to this list, or fix its call site.

		     NOT YET SOLVED — removing the interpreter entirely still needs EF
		     precompiled queries, and today it cannot be done. Measured on the
		     iPhone 17 simulator against this exact head, three separate aot-only
		     walls, each hidden behind the previous one:
		       1. DbContext construction — ClrPropertySetterFactory built a setter
		          per DbSet property. FIXED here: LoggingContext's DbSets are now
		          get-only (`=> Set<T>()`), so EF never builds those setters.
		       2. Model building — "Model building is not supported when publishing
		          with NativeAOT. Use a compiled model." Fixable with
		          `dotnet ef dbcontext optimize`; verified working, then reverted,
		          because wall 3 makes it useless on its own.
		       3. Every query — "Query wasn't precompiled and dynamic code isn't
		          supported with NativeAOT", from LoggingManager
		          .GetLoggingSessionsSnapshot. Needs the `precompile-queries` option
		          of that same command, which FAILS on this codebase: EF's
		          C#-to-LINQ translator rejects queries
		          that capture instance fields ("Encountered unknown identifier name
		          '_session'"), and the data layer is written that way throughout.
		     Clearing wall 3 means rewriting the data layer against an experimental
		     EF feature, with no test project here to catch a regression. See #102. -->
		<MtouchInterpreter>Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore,Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Relational,Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite,EFCore.BulkExtensions.Core,EFCore.BulkExtensions.Sqlite</MtouchInterpreter>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<!-- Pinned to the exact version the other heads use. A split Avalonia
		     graph is silent at compile time and throws MissingMethodException at
		     runtime; .github/scripts/check_avalonia_versions.py gates it. -->
		<PackageReference Include="Avalonia.iOS" Version="12.1.1" />
		<PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Fonts.Inter" Version="12.1.1" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<!-- App icon. Generated by daqifi-design-tokens/assets/app-icons — the
		     same artwork the Android, Windows and macOS heads use, so the app
		     looks identical wherever it is installed. Do not hand-edit; adjust
		     the source art and re-run that generator. Info.plist points at this
		     catalogue via XSAppIconAssets.

		     Contents.json is an ImageAsset, NOT None: actool is driven from the
		     @(ImageAsset) item group, and the .json files are the catalogue's
		     metadata — the idiom, size and filename of every image. Listed only as
		     None they are copied around but never handed to the asset compiler,
		     which is a SILENT failure: the build stays green and the app ships with
		     no icon. The step "iOS app bundle must contain a compiled asset
		     catalogue" in .github/workflows/build.yml is what proves it did run. -->
		<ImageAsset Include="Assets.xcassets\**\*.png" />
		<ImageAsset Include="Assets.xcassets\**\Contents.json" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<!-- The whole ported app rides in as a library, exactly as the Android
		     head takes it. Desktop-only transports (System.IO.Ports,
		     System.Management) restore their netstandard fallbacks and throw
		     PlatformNotSupported if touched — mobile is WiFi/TCP only by recorded
		     divergence (DIV-UI-003).
		     UndefineProperties: the managed app dll is platform-neutral — letting
		     the head's iOS RID flow into its build RID-flavors the obj tree and
		     breaks Avalonia's XamlIl task (AVLN9999). -->
		<ProjectReference Include="..\Daqifi.Avalonia\Daqifi.Avalonia.csproj"
		                  UndefineProperties="RuntimeIdentifier;SelfContained" />
	</ItemGroup>
</Project>
